esthesia. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.\n\n"},{"end_offset":1448,"heading":null,"page":1,"segment_id":"S000014","start_offset":1313,"table_hint":null,"text":"Meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials of perioperative dexmedetomidine to reduce delirium and mortality after cardiac surgery\n\n"},{"end_offset":1570,"heading":null,"page":1,"segment_id":"S000015","start_offset":1448,"table_hint":null,"text":"Robert D. Sanders 1,2,3,* , Jordan Wehrman 1 , Joanne Irons 1,2 , Jan Dieleman 4 , David Scott 5,6 and Yahya Shehabi 7,8\n\n"},{"end_offset":2223,"heading":null,"page":1,"segment_id":"S000016","start_offset":1570,"table_hint":null,"text":"1 University of Sydney, Camperdown, NSW, Australia, 2 Department of Anaesthetics, Royal Prince Alfred Hospital, Camperdown, NSW, Australia, 3 Institute of Academic Surgery, Camperdown, NSW, Australia, 4 Department of Anaesthesia & Perioperative Medicine, Westmead Hospital, Westmead, NSW, Australia, 5 Department of Anaesthesia & Acute Pain Medicine, St Vincent's Hospital, Melbourne, VIC, Australia, 6 University of Melbourne, Melbourne, VIC, Australia, 7 Monash Health School of Clinical Sciences, Monash University, Melbourne, VIC, Australia and 8 Prince of Wales Clinical School of Medicine, University of New South Wales, Randwick, NSW, Australia\n\n"},{"end_offset":2284,"heading":null,"page":1,"segment_id":"S000017","start_offset":2223,"table_hint":null,"text":"*Corresponding author. E-mail: robert.sanders@sydney.edu.au\n\n"},{"end_offset":2376,"heading":null,"page":1,"segment_id":"S000018","start_offset":2284,"table_hint":null,"text":"Keywords: cardiac surgery; delirium; dexmedetomidine; meta-analysis; perioperative outcome\n\n"},{"end_offset":3751,"heading":null,"page":1,"segment_id":"S000019","start_offset":2376,"table_hint":null,"text":"Editor d Recent studies 1,2 have not supported the hypothesis that perioperative dexmedetomidine improves outcomes from cardiac surgery via sympatholytic, anti-inflammatory or organ-protective effects. 3 Postoperative delirium has been a key area of recent interest. Two recent high-profile studies have not found significant effects on this endpoint, but show trends to effects on mortality. 1,2 Notably, the lack of effect on delirium may be attributable to over-sedation in one trial, evident by prolonged ventilation time in the dexmede tomidine-treated group. 2 That study 2 used methods that are excellent for detecting delirium in ventilated patients but suboptimal for non-intubated patients, 4 consistent with the low overall delirium rates detected. The other study 1 included both the Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) and CAM-ICU, which is a more comprehensive approach to delirium assessment. However, this study also showed no overall effects on in-hospital delirium but a trend to reduced delirium in the ICU. 1 This more short-term effect is likely more biologically plausible, but was not statistically proven. Intriguingly the study by Momeni and colleagues 1 suggested a potentially large effect on in-hospital mortality with 10-fold lower mortality in the dexmedetomidine group (for which the authors offer an appropriately conservative interpretation).\n\n"},{"end_offset":4395,"heading":null,"page":1,"segment_id":"S000020","start_offset":3751,"table_hint":null,"text":"As the delirium results seemed inconsistent with prior literature, and the mortality effects seemed surprisingly large, we conducted a hypothesis-generating meta-analysis of the available data from RCTs (based on other recent meta-analyses 5,6 updated with new studies 1,7 ). The meta-analysis was performed in R with the package 'metafor ' (R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria). Odds ratios were calculated for each article included in the analysis based on the incidence of delirium and mortality events from the total. Arandom effects model was fit to each outcome (delirium and mortality) both separately and combined.\n\n"},{"end_offset":4763,"heading":null,"page":1,"segment_id":"S000021","start_offset":4395,"table_hint":null,"text":"Our updated meta-analysis shows that delirium after cardiac surgery is reduced by 46% (odds ratio 0.54 [95% confidence interval 0.38 e 0.76]), however 30-day in-hospital mortality is not significantly reduced (odds ratio 0.55 [95% confidence interval 0.27 e 1.13], Fig. 1). Notable limitations for the metaanalysis include the heterogeneity of the results across the\n\n"},{"end_offset":4781,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000022","start_offset":4763,"table_hint":null,"text":"[DOCLING_PAGE 2]\n\n"},{"end_offset":4797,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000023","start_offset":4781,"table_hint":null,"text":"Correspondence\n\n"},{"end_offset":4803,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000024","start_offset":4797,"table_hint":null,"text":"e169\n\n"},{"end_offset":4806,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000025","start_offset":4803,"table_hint":null,"text":"-\n\n"},{"end_offset":5151,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000026","start_offset":4806,"table_hint":null,"text":"Fig 1. Forest plots showing meta-analyses of cardiac surgery studies comparing dexmedetomidine with a control for delirium and mortality. To facilitate a conservative interpretation, for Momeni and colleagues 1 in-hospital delirium rates were included. CI, confidence interval; Dex, dexmedetomidine; df, degrees of freedom; RE, random effects,\n\n"},{"end_offset":5168,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000027","start_offset":5151,"table_hint":null,"text":"Author and year\n\n"},{"end_offset":5189,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000028","start_offset":5168,"table_hint":null,"text":"Odds ratio [95% CI]\n\n"},{"end_offset":5213,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000029","start_offset":5189,"table_hint":null,"text":"Odds ratio (log scale)\n\n"},{"end_offset":5219,"heading":null,"page":2,"segment_id":"S000030","start_offset":5213,"table_hint":null,"text":"0.01\n\n"},{"end_offset":5225,"heading":null,"page":